:: StoryTitle
WORKING / non-canon — The Classical Plaque

:: Start
You are Iskel, garrison clerk of the Shotai post at Low Ferrow. Your job is counting: sacks, oaths, coins of occupation tax. This morning the paving crew calls you over to the forum. Under a cracked stone lies bronze, green at the edges.

The script is classical, the old Eld Lopesh hand. You read it slowly. *The ford and the upper ward, gifted to the House of Varrow forever, by the king's own word.* There is a seal. There is a king's name.

The moot of Low Ferrow has paid tax on that ford for thirty years.

---

[[Report it to Captain Durren|Captain]]
[[Show it to the old Feud-singer first|Singer]]

:: Captain
Captain Durren looks at the plaque the way he looks at a lame horse. "Bronze is bronze. Melt it. We need fittings."

On your way out, a Bedaru trader is waiting by the gate, Hollis by name, as if somebody had sent for him. "I hear you dug up a king," Hollis says. "Kings sell."

---

[[Melt it as ordered|Melt]]
[[Hear Hollis out|Bedaru]]
[[Take it to the Feud-singer instead|Singer]]

:: Singer
Old Maeve Corran sings the feud-lines at every moot. She runs her thumb over the king's name and her eyes go wet.

"My grandmother sang that the Varrows held the ford. Nobody believed her. Read this in the square, clerk, and every song in this ward gets truer by a hand."

---

[[Read it in the square|Read]]
[[Ask what the moot would do with it|Moot]]

:: Read
You stand on the forum steps at noon and read the plaque aloud, in the old script's sound, then in plain speech.

The square goes quiet, then very loud. Three families claim to be Varrows by dusk.

---

[[Stand by what it says|EndRead]]
[[Let Maeve sing it instead of you|EndSong]]

:: Melt
The forge-boy takes it with tongs. The king's name runs first, then the seal.

That night you find you remember every word. Memory, it turns out, is not a fitting.

---

[[Write it down in the garrison ledger|EndLedger]]
[[Say nothing, ever|EndMelt]]

:: Bedaru
Hollis offers more silver than your post pays in a year. "And you keep a rubbing, of course. A rubbing is just paper. Paper is safe."

---

[[Sell, and keep the rubbing|EndRubbing]]
[[Refuse and go to the moot|Moot]]

:: Moot
The moot elders meet in the cooper's loft so the garrison won't hear. They don't want a king. Kings bring captains with them. They want a quiet paper, something that halves the tax without anyone shouting.

"Help us forge a claim," says the eldest. "Something smaller. True enough. Hide the bronze."

---

[[Forge the quieter claim|EndForge]]
[[Refuse; the plaque says what it says|Read]]

:: EndRead
Durren arrests you, then releases you, because there's a crowd. The tax isn't lifted. But now every ward in the lowland is prying up its forum stones, looking for kings.

**END: The past, spoken aloud.**

[[Begin again|Start]]

:: EndSong
Maeve sings it, and a song can't be melted. Within a year the plaque is only a rumor and the song is canon in every tavern. Nobody asks whether the song got the king's name right.

**END: The past, sung.**

[[Begin again|Start]]

:: EndLedger
You write the inscription into the garrison ledger, in the occupier's book, under "salvage, bronze, one." A century later, a Lopadian scholar finds it there. The empire that melted the claim also kept it.

**END: Erased in metal, kept in ink.**

[[Begin again|Start]]

:: EndMelt
The fittings hold the gate hinges true for forty years. Nobody knows that the gate swings on a king's word.

**END: Melted.**

[[Begin again|Start]]

:: EndRubbing
Hollis sells the plaque in the south to a house that suddenly remembers it is Varrow. Your rubbing sits in a box. Every few years someone offers to buy it. You never decide whether you're keeping history or a lever.

**END: The past, privately held.**

[[Begin again|Start]]

:: EndForge
The forged claim is modest and believable, and it works: the tax on the ford is halved. The bronze stays under the cooper's floor. The moot now keeps two pasts: the true one hidden and the useful one filed.

**END: A quieter truth.**

[[Begin again|Start]]
